# Local configuration and secrets — never commit
#
# The settings files are named with a capital S because that is exactly what the library
# loads: JevEnvironment.GenericSettingsFile is "appSettings.json" and the machine override is
# "appSettings.{MACHINE_NAME}.json". A lowercase "appsettings.Local.json" pattern would NOT
# have matched either of them on a case-sensitive filesystem, so a developer's real key file
# named the way the library reads it would have been committed. Patterns are repeated in both
# cases so a file is protected whichever casing it was created with.
# The generic settings file is the one a user following the docs creates in their own app, so it is
# ignored everywhere — including a repo root, which is where a real key would end up committed.
appSettings.json
appsettings.json
appSettings.Local.json
appsettings.Local.json
appSettings.*.Local.json
appsettings.*.Local.json
appSettings.*.json
appsettings.*.json
# The tracked templates, re-included after the patterns above.
#
# Scoped to the sample directory on purpose. An unscoped "!appSettings.json" would un-ignore that
# file name at EVERY depth, including a repo-root appSettings.json — which is exactly where someone
# would put a real key and where it would then be committed. Only the shipped templates are
# re-included.
!samples/Jev.Sdk.Sample/appSettings.json
!samples/Jev.Sdk.Sample/.env.example
secrets.json
*.secrets.json
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.sample

# OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

